# Number rules for Hindi (hi-IN).
#
# Format: [section] headers, then one rule per line, fields separated by "|". "#" starts a comment.
# Hindi has an irregular word for every number from 1 to 99, so each is a row (kind "teen": a whole number below 100 that is not built from
# parts). Hundreds and the Indian scale words (sau, hazaar, laakh, karod, arab, kharab) multiply what comes before them: "teen sau pachaas" (350),
# "teen laakh pachaas hazaar" (350000). Digits are ASCII or Devanagari; the grouping is the Indian one (1,23,456), which is read as a number.
# Not supported: "sawa", "dedh", "dhai", "saadhe" before a number word (they are read before an hour, see datetime.rules).

[number.digits]
system | english
decimal | .
group | ,
negative | -−
native-digits | devanagari

